{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '1,180p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t\\documentclass{article}\n     2\t% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n     3\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n     4\t\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n     5\t\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n     6\t\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n     7\t\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n     8\t  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n     9\t  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n    10\t  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n    11\t  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n    12\t  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n    13\t  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n    14\t  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n    15\t  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n    16\t}\n    17\t% --- end watermark ---\n    18\t\\usepackage{iclr2026_conference,times}\n    19\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,graphicx,subcaption,longtable}\n    20\t\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n    21\t\\newcommand{\\E}{\\mathbb{E}}\n    22\t\\newcommand{\\Var}{\\operatorname{Var}}\n    23\t\\newcommand{\\clip}{\\operatorname{clip}}\n    24\t\\title{Frozen-Score Controls for Mean Inference:\\\\Width Gains and Coverage Limitations}\n    25\t\\author{}\n    26\t\n    27\t\\begin{document}\n    28\t\\maketitle\n    29\t\n    30\t\\begin{abstract}\n    31\tCan confidence intervals become narrower when labels, unlabeled observations, and the predictor are unchanged? We examine inference-side calibration of frozen scores for a binary population mean, using paired interval records from seven evaluation conditions and 256 repetitions per condition. The associated implementation fits honest, regularized score controls and returns a stabilized asymptotic interval; its bounded finite-sample construction is separate and unmeasured. Relative to the evaluated PPI++ comparator, mean width decreases in only one condition, by 0.000302, and increases in the other six. In the condition with population mean 0.0068, PPI++ coverage is 0.551 and 115 intervals collapse, whereas score-control coverage is 0.930 without collapse. However, the score-control width difference from exact-binomial inference is inconclusive, and pooled empirical coverage of 0.943 does not establish nominal validity. These results support condition-specific comparisons, not uniformly shorter valid intervals. Missing condition generators, sample-size metadata, and a matched calibration ablation limit reproducibility and mechanistic attribution.\n    32\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation]]\n    33\t% @evidence[[analysis_results.json#/cases]]\n    34\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L123-L192]]\n    35\t\\end{abstract}\n    36\t\n    37\t\\section{Introduction}\n    38\tScientific inference increasingly uses a small collection of expensive gold-standard labels together with predictions available on a larger collection of observations. The scientific target is often simpler than individual prediction: a prevalence, an average response, or another population summary. Prediction-powered inference uses labels to correct prediction error rather than treating machine predictions as truth \\citep{Angelopoulos2023PPI}. This distinction matters when the predictor is useful but imperfect. It also motivates a constrained question: once the observations and predictor are fixed, is there still room to improve the uncertainty attached to the population mean?\n    39\t\n    40\tThe constraint excludes acquiring labels, enlarging the unlabeled sample, or replacing the prediction model. It does not exclude fitting an inference-side transformation of its scalar output. A nonlinear transformation might make the score a better control variate for the outcome. Yet estimating this transformation consumes statistical information, and an interval can become shorter simply because its uncertainty calculation is inadequate. Thus, width alone cannot answer the question. Any efficiency claim must distinguish information extraction from estimation noise and must confront repeated-sampling coverage.\n    41\t\n    42\tWe study \\emph{honest frozen-score controls}: complementary labeled subsets fit a small regularized calibration family, while held-out labels correct its errors. A fixed-control variance identity explains the potential benefit of approximating the conditional outcome mean given the score. It also identifies a boundary on what calibration can accomplish: it cannot recover information absent from the frozen score. The evaluated interval uses a stabilized asymptotic variance approximation. A distinct bounded construction clarifies what a finite-sample argument would require, but its empirical efficiency is not observed here.\n    43\t\n    44\tOur central empirical finding is limited and asymmetric. Among seven conditions, only C2 has a negative paired mean-width difference relative to the evaluated PPI++ interval; the remaining six differences are positive. A low-prevalence condition exposes severe undercoverage and collapsed intervals for two asymptotic comparators. The score-control interval avoids these observed collapses but does not establish nominal coverage or an advantage over exact inference. These are comparisons of recorded intervals, not a causal test of nonlinear calibration.\n    45\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/case_summaries]]\n    46\t\n    47\tThe contributions are threefold: separating fixed-score efficiency potential from inference assumptions; a complete paired comparison retaining favorable and unfavorable conditions; and a failure analysis distinguishing non-collapse, narrowness, and validity. Missing generators and controlled ablations remain explicit limitations rather than being filled with inferred mechanisms.\n    48\t\n    49\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n    50\t\\centering\n    51\t\\begin{minipage}[c][1.8in][c]{\\textwidth}\n    52\t\\centering\n    53\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth,height=1.8in,keepaspectratio]{figures/frozen_score_overview.png}\n    54\t\\end{minipage}\n    55\t\\caption{Inference with unchanged observations and a frozen predictor. Complementary labeled folds fit score-only controls; held-out residual correction targets the population mean. The evaluated stabilized asymptotic interval and the separate, unmeasured bounded safety construction must not be conflated. No new labels or predictor training are part of this comparison.}\n    56\t\\label{fig:overview}\n    57\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L123-L192]]\n    58\t\\end{figure}\n    59\t\n    60\t\\section{Related Work}\n    61\t\\paragraph{Prediction-assisted estimation.}\n    62\tPPI supplies the central correction principle: combine predictions with a labeled estimate of their error \\citep{Angelopoulos2023PPI}. PPI++ adapts the contribution of predictions to improve statistical and computational efficiency under its assumptions \\citep{Angelopoulos2023PPIpp}. Our empirical baseline is the \\emph{evaluated PPI++ comparator}, not a claim about every implementation or theoretical formulation of PPI++. Classical control variates likewise exploit correlated auxiliary quantities to reduce Monte Carlo variance \\citep{Glasserman2004}. Survey calibration and model-assisted estimation use auxiliary information in a related spirit \\citep{DevilleSarndal1992,Sarndal1992}; their finite-population targets should not be silently substituted for the superpopulation target studied here. Missing-data theory provides additional context for assumptions connecting observed and unobserved outcomes \\citep{LittleRubin2019}.\n    63\t\n    64\t\\paragraph{Fitted controls and sample separation.}\n    65\tResidualization with estimated functions is familiar in semiparametric inference \\citep{Robinson1988,VanDerVaart1998}. Double/debiased machine learning combines orthogonal corrections with sample separation to control nuisance-estimation effects \\citep{Chernozhukov2018DML}. We use complementary training folds, but do not infer finite-sample validity from that structural similarity. Ridge regularization \\citep{HoerlKennard1970}, generalized cross-validation \\citep{Golub1979GCV}, and cross-validatory prediction assessment \\citep{Stone1974} motivate the archived calibration choices. A small linear-spline dictionary is a standard approximation device \\citep{Hastie2009}, not evidence that the dictionary matches the unknown conditional mean in these conditions.\n    66\t\n    67\t\\paragraph{Intervals near the boundary.}\n    68\tExact binomial inversion \\citep{BlythStill1983}, score-based intervals \\citep{Newcombe1998}, adjusted approximations \\citep{AgrestiCoull1998}, and analyses of erratic Wald coverage \\citep{BrownCaiDasGupta2001} motivate retaining an exact reference rather than comparing only against a normal approximation. A Wilson-style adjustment to a fitted-control estimator is not the binomial Wilson procedure. For bounded observations, concentration inequalities \\citep{Hoeffding1963} and empirical-Bernstein bounds \\citep{MaurerPontil2009} offer another route, at potentially substantial width cost. Variance-sensitive concentration also appears in sequential allocation \\citep{Audibert2009}; no active allocation is used here. The relevant distinction is between an assumption-dependent coverage argument and an observed coverage estimate, not between a favorable and unfavorable average width.\n    69\t\n    70\t\\section{Method}\n    71\t\\subsection{Target, Constraints, and the Efficiency Question}\n    72\tLet $(Y_i,S_i)_{i=1}^n$ denote labeled observations, where $Y_i\\in\\{0,1\\}$ and $S_i=f(X_i)\\in[0,1]$. The unlabeled observations contribute scores $(S_j^U)_{j=1}^N$. The predictor $f$ is fixed. The target is\n    73\t\\begin{equation}\n    74\t\\mu=\\E[Y],\\qquad\n    75\t\\Pr\\{\\mu\\in I\\}\\geq 1-\\alpha,\n    76\t\\label{eq:target}\n    77\t\\end{equation}\n    78\twith interval width $|I|$ as the efficiency criterion. The nominal level in the recorded interface is $1-\\alpha=0.95$. The method receives only labeled outcomes and labeled/unlabeled scores, not a population truth or condition identity.\n    79\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L1-L44]]\n    80\t\n    81\tFor the analysis of estimators, assume independent iid labeled pairs and iid unlabeled scores from the same population, with the predictor frozen independently of the inference labels. These are explicit statistical assumptions, not facts established by the unidentified condition generators. ``Fixed inputs'' means that compared methods have the same observations within a repetition. It does not mean conditioning away all repeated-sampling randomness, nor does it change the target to the realized mean of an observed finite pool. Different targets require different coverage arguments.\n    82\t\n    83\tThe practical question is whether a fitted transformation of $S$ can reduce width \\emph{without losing the coverage requirement}. We do not optimize the reported width over the observed repetitions. Instead, we compare the sealed interval outputs and retain coverage and width as separate outcomes. This prevents interpreting a narrow but under-covering interval as an efficiency solution.\n    84\t\n    85\t\\subsection{Design Rationale: What a Frozen Score Can Offer}\n    86\tFor a fixed square-integrable control $g$, write $\\rho=N/(n+N)$ and consider\n    87\t\\begin{equation}\n    88\t\\widehat\\mu_g=\\overline{Y-\\rho g(S)}_L+\\rho\\overline{g(S)}_U,\n    89\t\\quad\n    90\t\\Var(\\widehat\\mu_g)=\\frac{\\Var(Y-\\rho g(S))}{n}\n    91\t+\\frac{\\rho^2\\Var(g(S))}{N}.\n    92\t\\label{eq:fixed}\n    93\t\\end{equation}\n    94\tEqual score distributions in the labeled and unlabeled samples ensure cancellation of the control's expectation. There is no requirement that $g$ itself predict the outcome without bias. The finite-$N$ factor recognizes uncertainty in the unlabeled average rather than treating it as a known population moment.\n    95\t\n    96\tLet $m(S)=\\E[Y\\mid S]$. Expanding the variance in Equation~\\ref{eq:fixed} gives\n    97\t\\begin{align}\n    98\t\\Var(\\widehat\\mu_m)&=\\frac{\\E[\\Var(Y\\mid S)]}{n}\n    99\t+\\frac{\\Var(m(S))}{n+N},\\label{eq:oracle}\\\\\n   100\t\\Var(\\widehat\\mu_g)-\\Var(\\widehat\\mu_m)\n   101\t&=\\frac{\\rho}{n}\\Var\\{g(S)-m(S)\\}.\\label{eq:excess}\n   102\t\\end{align}\n   103\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/METHOD.md#Oracle-frontier-and-asymptotic-qualifications]]\n   104\tThe identity describes a population oracle among fixed controls, up to additive constants. It suggests a rationale for nonlinear calibration when the score--outcome relationship departs from an affine function. It is not a claim that the finite fitted dictionary attains this oracle. Nor is a variance identity automatically a confidence-interval theorem for a data-adaptive control. Constant or uninformative scores offer no auxiliary variance reduction in this calculation; labels still determine the irreducible outcome-noise term.\n   105\t\n   106\t\\subsection{Archived Honest Calibration Construction}\n   107\tThe inspectable implementation associated with the selected method uses two labeled folds and two unlabeled folds from independent permutations initialized by the repetition seed. For labeled fold $L_k$, the opposite labeled fold fits $g_{-k}$. Its intercept, feature centering, feature scaling, spline knots, and regularization choice all use that training fold only. Neither held-out scores nor unlabeled scores tune the calibration. After fitting, evaluated controls are clipped to $[0,1]$.\n   108\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L51-L102]]\n   109\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L123-L171]]\n   110\t\n   111\tThe family includes an intercept, a ridge affine function, and linear splines with training-score quantile knots. Up to three quartile knots become available at training size 16; up to seven quantile knots become available at size 64. The ridge penalties are $\\{0.001,0.01,0.1,1,10\\}$. Training-only GCV chooses a fit relative to the constant candidate. Constant-score or very small training samples retain the intercept. These choices specify the archived code, not measured hyperparameter sensitivities.\n   112\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L58-L93]]\n   113\t\n   114\tThe fold estimate and aggregate estimate are\n   115\t\\begin{equation}\n   116\t\\widehat\\mu_k=\\overline{Y-\\rho g_{-k}(S)}_{L_k}\n   117\t+\\rho\\overline{g_{-k}(S)}_{U_k},\\qquad\n   118\t\\widehat\\mu=\\sum_{k=1}^{2}w_k\\widehat\\mu_k,\\quad w_k=|L_k|/n.\n   119\t\\label{eq:fold}\n   120\t\\end{equation}\n   121\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L135-L172]]\n   122\tTraining separation prevents an observation's label from fitting its own control. It does not make the fitted folds unconditionally independent: a label held out in one fold trains the other fold's control. The implementation retains all held-out residuals and unlabeled control values for a pooled variance approximation. It also contains an affine-only matched function, but no measured affine-versus-spline ablation is available. Therefore, the empirical comparison concerns the complete interval construction.\n   123\t\n   124\t\\subsection{Returned Interval and Separate Safety Construction}\n   125\tLet $R_i=Y_i-\\rho g_{-k(i)}(S_i)$ and $Q_j=\\rho g_{-k(j)}(S_j^U)$. The archived asymptotic calculation is\n   126\t\\begin{equation}\n   127\t\\widehat V=\\frac{s_R^2}{n}+\\frac{s_Q^2}{N},\\quad\n   128\ta=\\frac{z_{1-\\alpha/2}^2}{n},\\quad\n   129\tc=\\frac{\\widehat\\mu+a/2}{1+a},\\quad\n   130\tr=\\frac{z_{1-\\alpha/2}\\sqrt{\\widehat V+z_{1-\\alpha/2}^2/(4n^2)}}{1+a}.\n   131\t\\label{eq:stabilized}\n   132\t\\end{equation}\n   133\tThe endpoints are $\\clip(c-r,0,1)$ and $\\clip(c+r,0,1)$. This boundary stabilization supplies a positive radius when the empirical variance vanishes, but it does not prove coverage for a fitted-control estimator. The pooled variance also approximates unequal fold weights when sample sizes are odd. For $n<16$, a sample-size-only rule instead returns a labeled-only Clopper--Pearson interval. This branch does not confer exact validity on the other sample sizes.\n   134\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L172-L185]]\n   135\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L246-L259]]\n   136\t\n   137\tThe same source computes a distinct empirical-Bernstein interval by bounding each held-out residual mean and unlabeled control mean. Conditional on training, their ranges are $[-\\rho,1]$ and $[0,\\rho]$. Componentwise bounds, a union bound, and the same fixed nonnegative fold weights give a finite-sample argument under the iid assumptions. Crucially, these endpoints are not returned by the primary interface, compared with the asymptotic endpoints, or selected by their widths. No coverage or width measurement for that safety construction is available. Supplementary Information supplies its argument and the fixed-control variance derivation.\n   138\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L109-L185]]\n   139\t\n   140\t\\section{Experimental Protocol and Evidence Scope}\n   141\t\\subsection{Paired Records and Comparators}\n   142\tThe completed sealed evaluation contains 256 seeded repetitions, with seven conditions in each repetition. We denote the conditions C1--C7, preserving their recorded identities. Every record supplies the truth, the score-control endpoints, and endpoints for evaluated PPI++, PPI, labeled-only asymptotic mean inference, labeled-only exact-binomial inference, and a full-range sanity interval. The inferential tables include all five nontrivial methods; $[0,1]$ is retained as a sanity reference rather than an efficiency headline. No scored repetition or unfavorable condition is removed.\n   143\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   144\t\n   145\tThe records omit condition generators, dataset names, per-condition $n,N$, raw scores, and label arrays. Recorded truths do not identify calibration nonlinearity, distribution shift, or predictor quality. All conditions are included, but no separately named transfer task is evidenced. These are evaluation-condition comparisons, not established cross-domain generalization.\n   146\t\n   147\tComparator versions and internal fitting details are unavailable; conclusions apply to the supplied endpoints. The archived calibration source and entrypoint are readable, but a separate final source tree is unavailable for file-by-file identity verification. Source-level method descriptions are therefore distinguished from sealed numerical results: neither a hash nor method-family alignment proves exact implementation identity.\n   148\t\n   149\t\\subsection{Uncertainty and Reporting Rules}\n   150\tFor each condition and method, we recompute width as upper minus lower and coverage as inclusion of the recorded truth in the closed interval. Coverage uncertainty is a pointwise equal-tailed exact-binomial interval for the repetition count. For a comparator $b$, define the paired difference\n   151\t\\begin{equation}\n   152\tD_r=|I_{\\mathrm{control},r}|-|I_{b,r}|,\\qquad\n   153\t\\overline D\\ \\pm\\ t_{0.975,R-1}\\,s_D/\\sqrt R.\n   154\t\\label{eq:paired}\n   155\t\\end{equation}\n   156\tHere $R$ is the number of repetitions. Negative differences mean narrower recorded intervals, not better coverage. The paired-$t$ interval summarizes mean differences; unlike exact-binomial coverage limits, it is approximate \\citep{Student1908}. The calculations reproduce the supplied condition summaries to numerical precision and use their endpoint records rather than repeatedly counting summaries attached only to the initial record.\n   157\t% @evidence[[scripts/build_artifacts.py]]\n   158\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/case_summaries]]\n   159\t\n   160\tThese intervals assume a repeated-sampling interpretation that distinct seeds alone do not verify. They are pointwise, not simultaneous. Multiplicity changes the question \\citep{BenjaminiHochberg1995}; no multiple-testing procedure or familywise gain claim is made. All comparisons are retained regardless of sign. Conditions within a seed may share randomness, so pooled coverage is descriptive, not a binomial estimate from independent condition--repetition pairs.\n   161\t\n   162\tThe sealed evaluation is recorded as completed and qualified. That procedural outcome does not certify exact nominal coverage, independence assumptions, adaptivity controls, or source identity. Candidate diagnostics are explicitly marked unverified in the evidence. No missing diagnostic is interpreted as a passed statistical test. Development scores and unscored alternatives are not used as scientific comparisons, and no new evaluation is run for this paper.\n   163\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n   164\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n   165\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/candidate_diagnostics_trust]]\n   166\t\n   167\t\\section{Results}\n   168\t\\subsection{Width Gains Depend on the Comparator and Condition}\n   169\tTables~\\ref{tab:coverage} and \\ref{tab:width} retain every condition and inferential comparator. Figure~\\ref{fig:comparison} places coverage next to paired differences so that apparent efficiency is not detached from uncertainty. In C2, the score-control mean width is $0.069770$, compared with $0.070072$ for evaluated PPI++. The paired difference is $-0.000302$, with pointwise 95\\% interval $[-0.000400,-0.000203]$. This is a small but resolved mean-width difference within that comparison. Both methods have empirical coverage $0.941406$, with exact-binomial interval $[0.905199,0.966838]$. Equal marginal coverage estimates do not show that they cover on the same repetitions or establish equal coverage probabilities.\n   170\t% @evidence[[analysis_results.json#/cases/1]]\n   171\t\n   172\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   173\t\\centering\n   174\t\\caption{Empirical coverage. Every condition has 256 repetitions and nominal coverage 0.95. Pointwise uncertainty is plotted in Figure~\\ref{fig:comparison} and fully tabulated in Supplementary Information. The low coverage of evaluated PPI++ and labeled normal inference in C5 remains visible.}\n   175\t\\label{tab:coverage}\n   176\t\\small\n   177\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n   178\t\\toprule\n   179\t\\input{tables/coverage.tex}\n   180\t\\end{tabular}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
