{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '226,265p'; nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '310,380p'\"","aggregated_output":"   226\t% @evidence[[analysis_results.json#/cases/4]]\n   227\t\n   228\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   229\t\\centering\n   230\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/rare_outcome_diagnostic.pdf}\n   231\t\\caption{C5 rare-outcome diagnostic, using all 256 endpoint records. (a) Empirical CDFs retain the mass at zero on a linear width axis. (b) Collapsed-interval counts. Labeled normal inference, omitted from this four-method display, also has 115 zero-width intervals. The associated coverage values appear in Table~\\ref{tab:coverage}. Avoiding collapse does not establish nominal coverage or superiority to exact-binomial inference.}\n   232\t\\label{fig:rare}\n   233\t% @evidence[[analysis_results.json#/cases/4]]\n   234\t\\end{figure}\n   235\t\n   236\t\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n   237\t\\paragraph{A possibility result is not an empirical guarantee.}\n   238\tEquation~\\ref{eq:excess} identifies why frozen-score transformation can help: it can reduce the discrepancy between a control and the conditional outcome mean without modifying the predictor. But the empirical evidence shows that this opportunity need not overcome fitting noise, fold effects, and interval stabilization. Even a genuinely favorable variance calculation would not establish coverage for the returned interval. The separate bounded construction makes this distinction explicit rather than retrospectively assigning its guarantee to an asymptotic output.\n   239\t\n   240\t\\paragraph{No isolated attribution to nonlinear calibration.}\n   241\tThe observed method differs from its comparators in calibration, sample separation, weighting, variance estimation, and boundary handling. There is no executed matched affine-only comparison, no observed oracle frontier, and no measured safety-track interval. Consequently, the C2 result is evidence about whole evaluated configurations. It cannot establish that splines caused the gain or that cross-fitting improved width. The unavailable controlled comparisons are missing evidence, not measured negative results.\n   242\t\n   243\t\\paragraph{Sampling and source provenance restrict interpretation.}\n   244\tThe iid common-population assumptions are central to both correction and the safety argument. The archive does not identify whether each generator satisfies them, whether sampling masks are independent, or whether population truths were constructed analytically or from a finite external pool. We retain the stipulated population target and recorded truths, while avoiding a stronger certification of the sampling design. Likewise, source hashes preserve provenance, but readable method-family alignment does not establish byte-level identity with the sealed executable. This limits attribution without erasing the recorded endpoint comparison.\n   245\t\n   246\t\\paragraph{Reproducibility is partial, not end-to-end.}\n   247\tThe included extraction script reproduces the numerical tables, plotted uncertainty, and width distributions from the read-only evidence registry. It does not recreate the unknown generators or rerun the original evaluation. The source archive and seeds document what is available, but missing $n,N$, data versions, and final implementation snapshot prevent an honest claim of full experimental reproducibility. There are no measured named-dataset results, verified transfer results, or robustness analyses beyond the recorded conditions. These omissions materially limit external validity.\n   248\t\n   249\t\\paragraph{Practical interpretation.}\n   250\tFor fixed-input comparisons, assess coverage before interpreting width. Paired differences remain meaningful when comparator widths vanish, and exact inference supplies an important boundary reference. Selecting an interval after observing its realized width requires its own coverage argument. Here, these principles reveal a modest local gain and substantial comparator dependence, not a generally preferable interval. They motivate controlled follow-up rather than a deployment recommendation.\n   251\t\n   252\t\\section{Conclusion}\n   253\tFrozen labels, unlabeled observations, and predictions do not mathematically eliminate all room for more efficient mean inference. A score-only control can exploit a better approximation to the conditional outcome mean; the fixed-control variance identity specifies this opportunity and the information it cannot recover. Whether a fitted procedure converts that opportunity into \\emph{valid, narrower intervals} is a separate empirical and theoretical question.\n   254\t\n   255\tThe paired records provide a bounded answer. Score control is slightly narrower than evaluated PPI++ in C2, but wider in the other six conditions. In the low-prevalence condition it avoids observed collapsed intervals and has much higher coverage than two asymptotic comparators, yet its width advantage over exact-binomial inference is unresolved and nominal validity remains unestablished. Missing calibration ablations prevent a component-level interpretation, and missing generation metadata prevent reconstruction of the complete study. The defensible conclusion is therefore condition-specific: inference-side flexibility may change width, but this evidence does not establish a uniformly shorter, statistically valid replacement for existing intervals under fixed inputs.\n   256\t% @evidence[[analysis_results.json#/cases]]\n   257\t\n   258\t\\clearpage\n   259\t\\bibliography{references}\n   260\t\\bibliographystyle{iclr2026_conference}\n   261\t\n   262\t\\clearpage\n   263\t\\appendix\n   264\t\\section{Supplementary Information}\n   265\t\\subsection{Complete Paired Interval Comparisons}\n   310\t\\midrule\n   311\tC6 & Score control & 0.945 [0.910, 0.970] & 0.16540 & 0 \\\\\n   312\tC6 & PPI++ & 0.930 [0.891, 0.958] & 0.16486 & 0 \\\\\n   313\tC6 & PPI & 0.930 [0.891, 0.958] & 0.16486 & 0 \\\\\n   314\tC6 & Label normal & 0.930 [0.891, 0.958] & 0.16486 & 0 \\\\\n   315\tC6 & Exact binomial & 0.949 [0.915, 0.973] & 0.17104 & 0 \\\\\n   316\t\\midrule\n   317\tC7 & Score control & 0.941 [0.905, 0.967] & 0.16727 & 0 \\\\\n   318\tC7 & PPI++ & 0.934 [0.896, 0.961] & 0.16459 & 0 \\\\\n   319\tC7 & PPI & 0.988 [0.966, 0.998] & 0.19287 & 0 \\\\\n   320\tC7 & Label normal & 0.930 [0.891, 0.958] & 0.16486 & 0 \\\\\n   321\tC7 & Exact binomial & 0.949 [0.915, 0.973] & 0.17104 & 0 \\\\\n   322\t\\midrule\n   323\t\\bottomrule\n   324\t\\end{longtable}\n   325\t\n   326\t\\begin{longtable}{llrr}\n   327\t\\caption{Paired score-control minus comparator width differences. Negative values indicate narrower recorded intervals, not a coverage-adjusted advantage.}\\label{tab:paired}\\\\\n   328\t\\toprule\n   329\tCondition & Comparator & Mean difference & Pointwise 95\\% limits\\\\\n   330\t\\midrule\n   331\t\\endfirsthead\n   332\t\\toprule\n   333\tCondition & Comparator & Mean difference & Pointwise 95\\% limits\\\\\n   334\t\\midrule\n   335\t\\endhead\n   336\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   337\tC1 & PPI++ & +0.002231 & [+0.001631, +0.002831] \\\\\n   338\tC1 & PPI & +0.001302 & [+0.000690, +0.001913] \\\\\n   339\tC1 & Label normal & -0.023034 & [-0.024169, -0.021900] \\\\\n   340\tC1 & Exact binomial & -0.029209 & [-0.030341, -0.028077] \\\\\n   341\t\\midrule\n   342\tC2 & PPI++ & -0.000302 & [-0.000400, -0.000203] \\\\\n   343\tC2 & PPI & -0.000644 & [-0.000742, -0.000547] \\\\\n   344\tC2 & Label normal & -0.012893 & [-0.013143, -0.012644] \\\\\n   345\tC2 & Exact binomial & -0.014651 & [-0.014900, -0.014401] \\\\\n   346\t\\midrule\n   347\tC3 & PPI++ & +0.003429 & [+0.002896, +0.003962] \\\\\n   348\tC3 & PPI & +0.002130 & [+0.001550, +0.002710] \\\\\n   349\tC3 & Label normal & -0.018195 & [-0.019163, -0.017227] \\\\\n   350\tC3 & Exact binomial & -0.024218 & [-0.025186, -0.023250] \\\\\n   351\t\\midrule\n   352\tC4 & PPI++ & +0.002452 & [+0.001972, +0.002933] \\\\\n   353\tC4 & PPI & -0.024381 & [-0.025514, -0.023247] \\\\\n   354\tC4 & Label normal & +0.002056 & [+0.001575, +0.002537] \\\\\n   355\tC4 & Exact binomial & -0.004025 & [-0.004505, -0.003544] \\\\\n   356\t\\midrule\n   357\tC5 & PPI++ & +0.022702 & [+0.021895, +0.023508] \\\\\n   358\tC5 & PPI & -0.018525 & [-0.021644, -0.015407] \\\\\n   359\tC5 & Label normal & +0.022565 & [+0.021741, +0.023389] \\\\\n   360\tC5 & Exact binomial & -0.000047 & [-0.000321, +0.000227] \\\\\n   361\t\\midrule\n   362\tC6 & PPI++ & +0.000537 & [+0.000179, +0.000894] \\\\\n   363\tC6 & PPI & +0.000537 & [+0.000179, +0.000894] \\\\\n   364\tC6 & Label normal & +0.000537 & [+0.000179, +0.000894] \\\\\n   365\tC6 & Exact binomial & -0.005638 & [-0.005995, -0.005281] \\\\\n   366\t\\midrule\n   367\tC7 & PPI++ & +0.002681 & [+0.002112, +0.003251] \\\\\n   368\tC7 & PPI & -0.025593 & [-0.026689, -0.024498] \\\\\n   369\tC7 & Label normal & +0.002409 & [+0.001840, +0.002978] \\\\\n   370\tC7 & Exact binomial & -0.003766 & [-0.004334, -0.003197] \\\\\n   371\t\\midrule\n   372\t\\bottomrule\n   373\t\\end{longtable}\n   374\t\n   375\tThe full-range interval is $[0,1]$ in the records and covers the recorded binary-mean truths. It is a check on the endpoint/target convention, not a competitive width baseline. Raw width ratios can be dominated by zero or near-zero denominators in C5. We therefore do not interpret the retained pooled ratio as a multiplicative efficiency advantage, and do not infer how any denominator regularization was implemented.\n   376\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   377\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/width_ratio_ppiplus]]\n   378\t\n   379\t\\subsection{Calibration and Interval Implementation}\n   380\t\\label{sec:implementation}\n","exit_code":