{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '421,440p' paper.tex; nl -ba source_evidence/calibration/program.py | sed -n '1,110p'; jq '.grounding_sources|with_entries(select(.key|startswith(\\\"final.\\\")))' paper_data.json\"","aggregated_output":"\\paragraph{Why fitted-control inference requires more.}\nFor fixed $g$, elementary variance algebra suffices. For estimated $g_{-k}$, conditional sample separation preserves the correction's expectation under the iid model, but unconditional covariances across folds remain. First-order inference requires additional control of fitted-function error and variance estimation, as emphasized more generally in semiparametric theory \\citep{Robinson1988,VanDerVaart1998,Chernozhukov2018DML}. The archive does not establish the required rates for its finite dictionary/GCV rule in each condition. The Efron--Stein perspective also emphasizes accounting for estimator sensitivity rather than treating fitted components as fixed \\citep{EfronStein1981}. We use no such inequality to certify the returned interval.\n\n\\paragraph{Bounded safety construction.}\nFor a held-out component sample $A_1,\\ldots,A_m$ with known range length $B$, the archived radius is\n\\begin{equation}\nb(A,\\delta)=\\min\\left\\{B,\\sqrt{\\frac{2s_A^2\\log(4/\\delta)}{m}}\n+\\frac{7B\\log(4/\\delta)}{3(m-1)}\\right\\},\\qquad m\\geq2.\n\\label{eq:eb}\n\\end{equation}\nFor a singleton, it uses $B$. This is a conservative two-sided empirical-Bernstein form \\citep{MaurerPontil2009}; known boundedness, as in classical concentration arguments \\citep{Hoeffding1963}, is essential. Capping at $B$ cannot invalidate a bound because both the sample and population means lie in the support interval. The variance-sensitive term can be useful, but the additive range term can make the interval substantially wider than an asymptotic one. Related variance-aware bounds are studied in other settings \\citep{Audibert2009}; those sequential results are not empirical evidence here.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L109-L119]]\n\nFor $K=2$ folds, each residual mean and each unlabeled control mean receives error probability $\\delta=\\alpha/(2K)$. Conditional on the opposite labeled training sample, the residuals have support $[-\\rho,1]$ and the unlabeled controls have support $[0,\\rho]$. Their expectations sum to $\\mu$. A union bound across the $2K$ component events therefore controls the probability of any failure by $\\alpha$, without requiring independence across the fitted folds. On this joint event, each fold interval contains $\\mu$; averaging lower and upper endpoints using fixed nonnegative weights summing to one preserves containment. Intersecting with $[0,1]$ preserves containment of a binary mean. The implementation replaces an empty numerical intersection by the full range.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/calibration/program.py#L129-L185]]\n\nThis is a coverage argument for the separate construction under the stated sampling and support assumptions. It is not an empirical coverage estimate, a width comparison, or a theorem for Equation~\\ref{eq:stabilized}. No width-dependent choice between the two constructions is justified by the union bound as implemented.\n\n\\subsection{Evidence Extraction and Reproducibility Boundaries}\n\\label{sec:reproduce}\n     1\t\"\"\"Frozen-predictor Bernoulli population-mean inference, CPU/binary64 only.\n     2\t\n     3\tRPC v1: handle(request) returns a finite JSON object. Only the exact documented\n     4\tkeys are accepted in responses; do not return diagnostics, paths, or state flags.\n     5\tThe predictor and samples are immutable. Fit inference-side transforms only.\n     6\tNever pool labels across episodes. No external files, network, or extra labels.\n     7\t\n     8\treset: {\"op\":\"reset\",\"seed\":nonnegative integer}, response {\"ok\":true}.\n     9\tAccept arbitrary seeds; they initialize RNG, never identify a workload/plane.\n    10\tClears all arrays, coefficients, results and randomness. Exactly once per episode.\n    11\t\n    12\tinput: {\"op\":\"input\",\"n\":integer>=2,\"N\":integer>=2,\"alpha\":0.05,\n    13\t\"dtype\":\"<f8\",\"encoding\":\"base64\",\"shape\":[2*n+N],\n    14\t\"total_bytes\":8*(2*n+N),\"chunk_count\":integer in [1,4],\n    15\t\"chunk_index\":integer in [0,chunk_count),\"offset\":integer,\"data\":base64 string}.\n    16\tThe logical byte stream concatenates Y_L[n],f_L[n],f_U[N] in this order.\n    17\tEach scalar is finite in [0,1]; Y_L is binary. n,N,alpha and metadata are\n    18\tplane-independent workload settings. No true mean, unlabeled labels, case name,\n    19\tbaseline, score, feedback or resource paths are supplied. Chunks are contiguous,\n    20\tordered, nonempty, at most 1 MiB decoded each, at most 4 MiB total. First input\n    21\tsets metadata; all later inputs repeat it exactly. No separate setup RPC is\n    22\tneeded: input #0 performs setup. Response {\"ok\":true,\"received_bytes\":integer}.\n    23\tAll four input calls are budgeted even when the actual value fits one chunk.\n    24\tAssembly is exclusively in candidate-owned RAM. No spill or shared paths.\n    25\t\n    26\tinfer: {\"op\":\"infer\"}, only after every input chunk, exactly once per episode.\n    27\tCommits the input stream and fits the actual method in one operation. Response\n    28\t{\"estimate\":finite number,\"lower\":finite number,\"upper\":finite number}, with\n    29\t0<=lower<=upper<=1 and 0<=estimate<=1. Closed intervals target E[Y] at 1-alpha.\n    30\tNo additional response fields. Endpoints need not contain the point estimate.\n    31\tAfter infer only reset is legal. No state export/import or extra probes exist.\n    32\tEvery complete message including envelope/framing must be <64 MiB (design cap\n    33\t8 MiB; endpoint/ack responses <16 KiB); keep input logical total <=4 MiB.\n    34\t\n    35\tCandidate: two-fold honest, clipped, regularized linear-spline score controls.\n    36\tAll knots and tuning use complementary labeled folds only. Unlabeled scores\n    37\tare split too; the pooling coefficient is N/(n+N). The RPC returns a stabilized Wald\n    38\ttrack for n>=16 and the labeled-only exact interval for n<16, a size-only rule.\n    39\tThe independently computed bounded empirical-Bernstein track is internal and\n    40\tnever used to select a narrower interval. Wald coverage is only asymptotic;\n    41\tneither cross-fitting nor a small-sample exact branch makes it finite-sample\n    42\tvalid at other sizes. No empirical coverage or width improvement is asserted.\n    43\t\"\"\"\n    44\timport base64\n    45\timport math\n    46\t\n    47\timport numpy as np\n    48\t\n    49\t_state = None\n    50\t\n    51\t\n    52\tclass ScoreCalibrator:\n    53\t    \"\"\"Small ridge linear-spline family, tuned exclusively on its fit sample.\"\"\"\n    54\t\n    55\t    def __init__(self, nonlinear=True):\n    56\t        self.nonlinear = nonlinear\n    57\t\n    58\t    def fit(self, scores, outcomes):\n    59\t        train_scores = np.asarray(scores, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\n    60\t        train_outcomes = np.asarray(outcomes, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\n    61\t        self.intercept = float(np.mean(train_outcomes))\n    62\t        self.knots = np.empty(0)\n    63\t        self.coefficients = np.empty(0)\n    64\t        self.feature_mean = np.empty(0)\n    65\t        self.feature_scale = np.empty(0)\n    66\t        sample_count = train_scores.size\n    67\t        if sample_count < 4 or np.ptp(train_scores) < 1e-12:\n    68\t            return self\n    69\t        centered_outcomes = train_outcomes - self.intercept\n    70\t        best_gcv = float(np.mean(centered_outcomes**2) / (1.0 - 1.0 / sample_count)**2)\n    71\t        knot_families = [np.empty(0)]\n    72\t        if self.nonlinear and sample_count >= 16:\n    73\t            knot_families.append(np.unique(np.quantile(train_scores, [0.25, 0.5, 0.75])))\n    74\t        if self.nonlinear and sample_count >= 64:\n    75\t            knot_families.append(np.unique(np.quantile(train_scores, np.linspace(0.125, 0.875, 7))))\n    76\t        for knots in knot_families:\n    77\t            features = self._basis(train_scores, knots)\n    78\t            feature_mean = features.mean(axis=0)\n    79\t            feature_scale = features.std(axis=0)\n    80\t            feature_scale = np.maximum(feature_scale, 1e-8)\n    81\t            centered_features = (features - feature_mean) / feature_scale\n    82\t            gram = centered_features.T @ centered_features / sample_count\n    83\t            cross = centered_features.T @ centered_outcomes / sample_count\n    84\t            identity = np.eye(gram.shape[0])\n    85\t            for penalty in (0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 1.0, 10.0):\n    86\t                coefficient = np.linalg.solve(gram + penalty * identity, cross)\n    87\t                residuals = centered_outcomes - centered_features @ coefficient\n    88\t                degrees = 1.0 + float(np.trace(np.linalg.solve(gram + penalty * identity, gram)))\n    89\t                denominator = max(1.0 - degrees / sample_count, 1.0 / sample_count)\n    90\t                gcv = float(np.mean(residuals**2) / denominator**2)\n    91\t                if gcv < best_gcv:\n    92\t                    best_gcv = gcv\n    93\t                    self.knots = knots.copy()\n    94\t                    self.coefficients = coefficient\n    95\t                    self.feature_mean = feature_mean\n    96\t                    self.feature_scale = feature_scale\n    97\t        return self\n    98\t\n    99\t    @staticmethod\n   100\t    def _basis(scores, knots):\n   101\t        return np.column_stack((scores, np.maximum(scores[:, None] - knots[None, :], 0.0)))\n   102\t\n   103\t    def predict(self, scores):\n   104\t        scores = np.asarray(scores, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\n   105\t        if not self.coefficients.size:\n   106\t            return np.full(scores.size, self.intercept)\n   107\t        features = (self._basis(scores, self.knots) - self.feature_mean) / self.feature_scale\n   108\t        return self.intercept + features @ self.coefficients\n   109\t\n   110\t\n{\n  \"final.evidence.episodes\": {\n    \"candidate_diagnostics_trust\": \"unverified\",\n    \"episode_count\": 256,\n    \"evaluation_scope\": \"sealed_final\",\n    \"id\": \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n    \"json_pointer\": \"/episodes\",\n    \"manifest_path\": \"work/results/t10/codex/20261107_014149/final_evaluation/final_manifest.json\",\n    \"path\": \"work/results/t10/codex/20261107_014149/final_evaluation/results/evaluation_evidence.json\",\n    \"program_tree_sha256\": \"sha256:980ddc8e93b7f07cab282a238d5934762740a6ace7b280c34d278de79a94c00b\",\n    \"results_tree_sha256\": \"sha256:3e3e28257a86eea7abe1bb58e9e2b3e125afb7bee8b4a5ae9b11a1bdb234df2c\",\n    \"sha256\": \"sha256:94e3af7d21a2cd42b86ec4ce762827b81355177a2df306b374a76537d58cf943\",\n    \"type\": \"evaluation_evidence\"\n  },\n  \"final.metric.combined_score\": {\n    \"evaluation_scope\": \"sealed_final\",\n    \"id\": \"final.metric.combined_score\",\n    \"manifest_path\": \"work/results/t10/codex/20261107_014149/final_evaluation/final_manifest.json\",\n    \"results_tree_sha256\": \"sha256:3e3e28257a86eea7abe1bb58e9e2b3e125afb7bee8b4a5ae9b11a1bdb234df2c\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\",\n    \"value\": 0.8701122015120599\n  },\n  \"final.metric.control_pass_rate\": {\n    \"evaluation_scope\": \"sealed_final\",\n    \"id\": \"final.metric.control_pass_rate\",\n    \"manifest_path\": \"work/results/t10/codex/20261107_014149/final_evaluation/final_manifest.json\",\n    \"results_tree_sha256\": \"sha256:3e3e28257a86eea7abe1bb58e9e2b3e125afb7bee8b4a5ae9b11a1bdb234df2c\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\",\n    \"value\": 1.0\n  },\n  \"final.metric.correct\": {\n    \"evaluation_scope\": \"sealed_final\",\n    \"id\": \"final.met