{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^#{1,4} '\"' paper_evidence_audit_packet.md; jq -r '.references[]|[.id//.source_id//.key//\\\"\\\",.title//\\\"\\\",.consultation_scope//.consultation//\\\"\\\",.evidence_path//.local_path//\\\"\\\"]|@tsv' references_provenance.json; jq '.summary' scientific-evidence/verification-report.json; sed -n '219,226p' paper.tex; sed -n '75,96p' source_evidence/calibration/program.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^#{1,4} '\"' paper_evidence_audit_packet.md; jq -r '.references[]|[.id//.source_id//.key//\\\"\\\",.title//\\\"\\\",.consultation_scope//.consultation//\\\"\\\",.evidence_path//.local_path//\\\"\\\"]|@tsv' references_provenance.json; jq '.summary' scientific-evidence/verification-report.json; sed -n '219,226p' paper.tex; sed -n '75,96p' source_evidence/calibration/program.py\"","aggregated_output":"1:# Agentic evidence audit packet\n9:## Expected audit focus\n18:## Artifact checklist\n27:## validation report summary\n46:## scientific-evidence verification summary\n62:## claims_grounding excerpt\n356:## paper_data summary\n379:## references provenance summary\n691:## manuscript source excerpt\n958:## supplementary information excerpt\nref.doi.10.1080/00031305.1998.10480550\tApproximate is Better than “Exact” for Interval Estimation of Binomial Proportions\tAbstract, not full paper\t\nref.doi.10.1126/science.adi6000\tPrediction-powered inference\tSelected primary PDF pages\t\nref.arxiv.2311.01453\tPPI++: Efficient Prediction-Powered Inference\tSelected primary PDF pages\t\nref.doi.10.1016/j.tcs.2009.01.016\tExploration–exploitation tradeoff using variance estimates in multi-armed bandits\tHAL abstract for the exact historical work\t\nref.doi.10.1111/j.2517-6161.1995.tb02031.x\tControlling the False Discovery Rate: A Practical and Powerful Approach to Multiple Testing\tAbstract, not full paper\t\nref.doi.10.1080/01621459.1983.10477938\tBinomial Confidence Intervals\tAbstract, not full paper\t\nref.doi.10.1214/ss/1009213286\tInterval Estimation for a Binomial Proportion\tAbstract, not full paper\t\nref.doi.10.1111/ectj.12097\tDouble/debiased machine learning for treatment and structural parameters\tAbstract, not full paper\t\nref.doi.10.1080/01621459.1992.10475217\tCalibration Estimators in Survey Sampling\tAbstract, not full paper\t\nref.doi.10.1214/aos/1176344552\tBootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife\tAbstract, not full paper\t\nref.doi.10.1214/aos/1176345462\tThe Jackknife Estimate of Variance\tAbstract, not full paper\t\nref.doi.10.1007/978-1-4899-4541-9\tAn Introduction to the Bootstrap\tPublisher synopsis\t\nref.doi.10.1007/978-0-387-21617-1\tMonte Carlo Methods in Financial Engineering\tPublisher synopsis\t\nref.doi.10.1080/00401706.1979.10489751\tGeneralized Cross-Validation as a Method for Choosing a Good Ridge Parameter\tAbstract, not full paper\t\nref.doi.10.1007/978-0-387-84858-7\tThe Elements of Statistical Learning\tPublisher synopsis; full text unavailable\t\nref.doi.10.1080/01621459.1963.10500830\tProbability Inequalities for Sums of Bounded Random Variables\tAbstract, not full paper\t\nref.doi.10.1080/00401706.1970.10488634\tRidge Regression: Biased Estimation for Nonorthogonal Problems\tAbstract, not full paper\t\nref.doi.10.1002/9781119013563\tStatistical Analysis with Missing Data\tAbstract, not full paper\t\nref.arxiv.0907.3740\tEmpirical Bernstein Bounds and Sample Variance Penalization\tPrimary PDF pages including empirical-Bernstein theorem\t\nref.doi.10.1002/(sici)1097-0258(19980430)17:8<857::aid-sim777>3.0.co;2-e\tTwo-sided confidence intervals for the single proportion: comparison of seven methods\tAbstract, not full paper\t\nref.doi.10.2307/1912705\tRoot-N-Consistent Semiparametric Regression\tAbstract, not full paper\t\nref.doi.10.1007/978-1-4612-4378-6\tModel Assisted Survey Sampling\tBook synopsis\t\nref.doi.10.1111/j.2517-6161.1974.tb00994.x\tCross-Validatory Choice and Assessment of Statistical Predictions\tAbstract, not full paper\t\nref.doi.10.1093/biomet/6.1.1\tThe Probable Error of a Mean\tAbstract, not full paper\t\nref.doi.10.1017/cbo9780511802256\tAsymptotic Statistics\tAbstract, not full paper\t\n{\n  \"anchored_references\": 69,\n  \"dangling_references\": 0,\n  \"manifests_checked\": 14,\n  \"resolved_references\": 47,\n  \"total_references\": 116\n}\n\\subsection{Low-Prevalence Failure Analysis}\nC5 has recorded truth $\\mu=0.0068$. Evaluated PPI++ and labeled normal intervals each cover in $0.550781$ of repetitions, with pointwise limits $[0.487609,0.612764]$. Each has 115 zero-width intervals. Score control has no zero-width intervals and coverage $0.929688$; evaluated PPI has two collapsed intervals and coverage $0.941406$. Exact-binomial coverage is $0.996094$, with limits $[0.978429,0.999901]$.\n% @evidence[[analysis_results.json#/cases/4]]\n\nFigure~\\ref{fig:rare} shows the width distribution rather than only a ratio to the narrow comparator. The mass at zero and low coverage invalidate an interpretation of the PPI++ mean width as a stand-alone efficiency achievement in this condition. They do not, by themselves, identify every internal cause of a coverage miss. Boundary stabilization is a plausible source-level reason to expect fewer mechanical collapses, but the final records lack a matched stabilization ablation and a separately verified source snapshot.\n\nThe score-control and exact-binomial mean widths are $0.038898$ and $0.038945$, respectively. Their paired difference is $-0.000047$, with interval $[-0.000321,0.000227]$. Therefore, the records do not resolve a width advantage over exact inference in the condition where severe asymptotic undercoverage is most visible. The exact interval also has higher observed coverage. These observations defeat a simple ``repair collapse while becoming narrower'' interpretation.\n% @evidence[[analysis_results.json#/cases/4]]\n            knot_families.append(np.unique(np.quantile(train_scores, np.linspace(0.125, 0.875, 7))))\n        for knots in knot_families:\n            features = self._basis(train_scores, knots)\n            feature_mean = features.mean(axis=0)\n            feature_scale = features.std(axis=0)\n            feature_scale = np.maximum(feature_scale, 1e-8)\n            centered_features = (features - feature_mean) / feature_scale\n            gram = centered_features.T @ centered_features / sample_count\n            cross = centered_features.T @ centered_outcomes / sample_count\n            identity = np.eye(gram.shape[0])\n            for penalty in (0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 1.0, 10.0):\n                coefficient = np.linalg.solve(gram + penalty * identity, cross)\n                residuals = centered_outcomes - centered_features @ coefficient\n                degrees = 1.0 + float(np.trace(np.linalg.solve(gram + penalty * identity, gram)))\n                denominator = max(1.0 - degrees / sample_count, 1.0 / sample_count)\n                gcv = float(np.mean(residuals**2) / denominator**2)\n                if gcv < best_gcv:\n                    best_gcv = gcv\n                    self.knots = knots.copy()\n                    self.coefficients = coefficient\n                    self.feature_mean = feature_mean\n                    self.feature_scale = feature_scale\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
